{% extends "pages/_docs_guide.jinja" %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "_api.jinja" as api %}
{% from "data/docs.jinja" import docs_tested %}
{% set guide_slug = 'output' %}
{% block guide_body %}
<section aria-labelledby="capture">
  <h2 id="capture">Capture and echo</h2>
  <p>
    Capture is on and echo is off by default. With capture on,
    <code>stdout</code> and <code>stderr</code> on the result are decoded
    strings; with it off, they are <code>None</code>. Echo mirrors output to
    the parent's sinks and can be set per stream. The two are independent:
    a stream can be captured silently, or echoed without being kept.
  </p>
  <p>
    Echo limits each mirrored line to 64 KiB by default, counting its
    truncation marker and terminator; captured output is always complete.
    Set <code>max_echo_line_bytes=None</code> only where unbounded mirroring
    is safe. {{ api.ref('CommandResult') }}<code>.relay_fallbacks</code>
    records any echo that fell back, by stream and closed category and
    without the output itself; see
    <a href="#sink-fails">when an echo destination fails</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="lines">
  <h2 id="lines">Observe each line</h2>
  <p>
    {{ api.ref('RunOutputOptions') }}<code>.on_line</code> receives every
    decoded line as a {{ api.ref('LineEvent') }}, even when capture and echo
    are both off. The callback runs synchronously and should return
    promptly. Lines keep their order within a stream, not across streams.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('output_lines.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="output")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
lines = []
result = python("-c", "print('one'); print('two')").run_sync(
    output=RunOutputOptions(capture=False, on_line=lines.append),
)
assert result.ok and result.stdout is None
assert [(line.stream, line.text) for line in lines] == [
    ("stdout", "one"),
    ("stdout", "two"),
]
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="stream">
  <h2 id="stream">Stream lines as they arrive</h2>
  <p>
    When a caller must react before the command finishes, iterate
    {{ api.ref('SafeCmd.lines') }}, an async iterator with backpressure. Use
    it as an async context manager whenever the loop can stop early: leaving
    the block closes the stream and tears the child down, as a cancelled
    <code>run()</code> would. Merely breaking out of the loop does not.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('first_line.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import asyncio
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="follow")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
command = python(
    "-c", "import time\nfor n in range(100):\n print(n, flush=True); time.sleep(0.01)"
)


async def first_line() -> str:
    async with command.lines() as stream:
        async for event in stream:
            # Leaving the block closes the stream and stops the child.
            return event.text
    raise AssertionError("the child should print at least one line")


assert asyncio.run(first_line()) == "0"
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    After a loop that runs to the end, <code>stream.result</code> holds the
    same {{ api.ref('CommandResult') }} that <code>run()</code> would have
    returned. Pipelines have no <code>lines()</code>; <code>on_line</code> on a
    pipeline run observes the final stage's stdout and every stage's
    stderr, since interior stdout feeds the next stage instead.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="sink-fails">
  <h2 id="sink-fails">When an echo destination fails</h2>
  <p>
    A sink can fail while echoing: a text-only console may be unable to
    encode the child's output, or the reader at the other end of a pipe may
    go away. A sink that cannot encode is always handled: echo stops for that
    stream while capture completes. A sink whose destination has closed is
    handled only when the run opts in, with
    {{ api.ref('BrokenPipePolicy') }}<code>.BEST_EFFORT</code> on
    {{ api.ref('RunOutputOptions') }}:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('broken_pipe.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import io
import sys

from cuprum import (
    BrokenPipePolicy,
    ExecutionContext,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    RunOutputOptions,
    sh,
)


class ClosedReader(io.TextIOBase):
    """An echo destination whose reader has gone away."""

    def write(self, text: str) -> int:
        raise BrokenPipeError


catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="broken-pipe")
command = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)("-c", "print('hello')")
result = command.run_sync(
    output=RunOutputOptions(echo=True, broken_pipe_policy=BrokenPipePolicy.BEST_EFFORT),
    context=ExecutionContext(stdout_sink=ClosedReader()),
)
assert result.ok and result.stdout == "hello\n"
assert [(f.stream, f.error_category) for f in result.relay_fallbacks] == [
    ("stdout", "broken_pipe")
]
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    Under best-effort, the <code>BrokenPipeError</code> from the sink disables
    echo for the affected stream only; capture, line observation, and child
    reaping continue, and the result's <code>relay_fallbacks</code> records
    one entry naming the stream and the <code>broken_pipe</code> category,
    never the output. The default, <code>STRICT</code>,
    propagates the error and aborts the run. Only <code>BrokenPipeError</code>
    is affected; any other sink <code>OSError</code> propagates under both
    policies.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="quiet">
  <h2 id="quiet">A heartbeat for a quiet child</h2>
  <p>
    <code>RunOutputOptions(idle_after=30.0)</code> writes a notice to the
    stderr sink after 30 seconds without output, then once per further
    silent interval; the default is off. The notice is never mixed into
    captured output. <code>on_idle(total, idle)</code> replaces it. A
    heartbeat reports silence, not a deadlock, and changes neither the
    deadline nor the exit status. Keep the callback and any sink it writes
    to prompt: both run on the command's event loop.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('heartbeat.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="idle")
command = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)("-c", "print('done')")
result = command.run_sync(output=RunOutputOptions(idle_after=30.0))
assert result.ok and result.stdout == "done\n"
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="redirect">
  <h2 id="redirect">Send a stream to a file or the parent</h2>
  <p>
    Capture keeps output in memory for the caller to read. When output should
    land somewhere else, such as a log file or the parent's own terminal,
    redirect the stream instead. {{ api.ref('RunOutputOptions') }}<code>.stdout</code>
    and <code>.stderr</code> accept a {{ api.ref('StdioTarget') }}, and the
    four kinds differ in who owns the destination.
  </p>
  {% call ui.matrix('stdio-targets-caption', 'Standard-stream targets', ['Target', 'The child\'s stream goes to', 'Owned by']) %}
  {% for target, destination, owner in [
    ('StdioTarget.pipe()', 'A pipe Cuprum reads, for capture, echo, line observation, or a heartbeat.', 'Cuprum'),
    ('StdioTarget.inherit()', 'The parent\'s own stream, passed straight through.', 'Parent'),
    ('StdioTarget.path(p)', 'The file at p, opened just before the spawn and closed just after.', 'Cuprum'),
    ('StdioTarget.fd(f)', 'A descriptor or open file object; flushed before the spawn, never closed.', 'Caller'),
  ] %}
  <tr role="row">
    <th scope="row" role="rowheader"><code>{{ target }}</code></th>
    {% call ui.matrix_cell('The child\'s stream goes to') %}{{ destination }}{% endcall %}
    {% call ui.matrix_cell('Owned by') %}{{ owner }}{% endcall %}
  </tr>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endcall %}
  <p>
    When a stream is left unset, Cuprum uses a pipe if something needs to
    read it and discards the output otherwise, so <code>inherit()</code> is
    the way to let an unobserved stream reach the parent's terminal. Any
    target other than <code>pipe()</code> counts as a redirect, and capture
    is on by default, so pass <code>capture=False</code> when redirecting
    stdout:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('redirect_stdout.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, StdioTarget, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="redirect")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
log = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp()) / "out.log"
result = python("-c", "print('redirected')").run_sync(
    output=RunOutputOptions(capture=False, stdout=StdioTarget.path(log)),
)
assert result.stdout is None, "a redirected stream is not captured"
assert log.read_text(encoding="utf-8") == "redirected\n"
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    A few combinations are contradictory, and Cuprum rejects them with
    <code>ValueError</code> before the child starts rather than silently
    choosing one side:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Capture or echo with a redirected stream, including <code>inherit()</code>.</strong> Both read from a pipe, and a redirected stream has none. Line observation and the heartbeat are not rejected, but they see nothing from a redirected stream.</li>
    <li><strong>One path for both stdout and stderr.</strong> Each open starts at offset 0, so the two streams would overwrite each other. Use two paths, or one <code>fd()</code> target the caller manages.</li>
    <li><strong>A redirected stdout with <code>lines()</code>, or either stream with <code>start()</code>.</strong> Both read the child's output in the parent. Use <code>run()</code> or <code>run_sync()</code> instead.</li>
    <li><strong>An inherited stdin alongside a stdin source.</strong> <code>RunOutputOptions(stdin=StdioTarget.inherit())</code> with <code>stdin=StdinInput(...)</code> or <code>stdin=StdinStream(...)</code> gives two answers to where the input comes from; this is checked when the run starts. A <code>pipe()</code> stdin target with a source is accepted, because the source is written through that pipe.</li>
    <li><strong>Targets on a <code>Pipeline</code>.</strong> Redirect a single command instead.</li>
    <li><strong><code>path()</code> or <code>fd()</code> on Windows.</strong> File and descriptor redirection needs POSIX descriptors; <code>pipe()</code> and <code>inherit()</code> work everywhere.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Reusing one <code>fd()</code> target across several runs shares its file
    offset, so each run continues where the previous one stopped.
    <code>RunOutputOptions.stdin</code> accepts only <code>pipe()</code> and
    <code>inherit()</code>: the input itself is passed to the run call's
    <code>stdin=</code> argument, as
    <a href="/cuprum/docs/guides/input-and-deadlines/#stream">Stream input from a producer</a>
    describes.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="binary">
  <h2 id="binary">Binary output</h2>
  <p>
    Capture decodes by default, so <code>stdout</code> and <code>stderr</code>
    are strings, and a byte the child wrote that is not valid UTF-8 arrives
    as <code>U+FFFD</code>. That is right for text and wrong for an archive
    or an image, because a decoded capture cannot be turned back into the
    bytes the child wrote. {{ api.ref('SafeCmd.run_bytes') }} and
    <code>Pipeline.run_bytes()</code>, with <code>run_bytes_sync()</code>
    counterparts, are the same runs with capture left undecoded:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('binary_output.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="binary-output")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
payload = bytes(range(256)) + b"\xff\x00\xfe\x80"
result = python(
    "-c", f"import sys; sys.stdout.buffer.write({payload!r})"
).run_bytes_sync()
assert result.ok
assert result.stdout == payload
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    The byte-mode entry points take the same <code>output</code>,
    <code>timeout</code>, <code>context</code>, and, for a single command,
    <code>stdin</code> arguments, and return {{ api.ref('BytesCommandResult') }}
    or {{ api.ref('BytesPipelineResult') }}, which declare the same fields as
    their text counterparts with <code>stdout</code> and <code>stderr</code>
    as <code>bytes | None</code>. The mode is explicit: <code>run()</code>
    never returns bytes and <code>run_bytes()</code> never returns text. A
    timeout still carries its partial output in the same field,
    <code>capture=False</code> still yields <code>None</code>, and an
    external cancellation still re-raises <code>asyncio.CancelledError</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Binary mode covers capture. A caller-supplied <code>on_line</code> is
    rejected with <code>ValueError</code> before the child is spawned, since a
    callback carrying decoded text would be a second, contradictory contract
    for the same stream. Echo, sinks, and the heartbeat are unaffected, and
    hooks registered with {{ api.ref('observe') }} still receive decoded
    lines: undecodable bytes are replaced in the event while the captured
    payload keeps the child's own bytes. That replacement is scoped to byte
    mode; a text-mode run under <code>errors="strict"</code> still raises
    <code>UnicodeDecodeError</code> as before.
  </p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
